<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Gengenbach</h1><p class="st-meta">1360 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Gengenbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Black Forest imperial city in the Kinzig valley that grew around a Benedictine abbey and kept its freedom until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gengenbach grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in the 8th century. The town received its rights under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and in the 14th century became a free imperial city, recognised by Charles IV in 1360. With <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-zell-am-harmersbach">Zell am Harmersbach</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-offenburg">Offenburg</a> it lay within the imperial bailiwick of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a>, which emperors often pledged to neighbouring princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> governed the town, whose economy rested on wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, crafts and traffic along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinzig-rhine">Kinzig</a> valley route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>. Relations with the abbey were often tense. Gengenbach briefly accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> but returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. French troops burned it in 1689 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, and it was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the mediatisation gave Gengenbach and its neighbours to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a> in 1806. Its half-timbered old town, walls and town hall survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1360 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)">